y" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045593/state.json" "file_sha256" "2441410f40421933fd9f2904bef98dac387611ebb758a09108a0854c3954d2f0"} {"question" "q-135392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inverse of the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045593/state.json" "file_sha256" "2441410f40421933fd9f2904bef98dac387611ebb758a09108a0854c3954d2f0"} {"question" "q-135392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we're given a Frobenius matrix:\n\n- all entries on the main diagonal are ones\n\n- the matrix has only one column (the $1$st column) where some entries below the main diagonal are not zeros\n\n- all the other entries outside the main diagonal are zeros\n\nTo find the inverse of a Frobenius matrix, we simply take the non-zero entries outside the main diagonal with opposite signs. \n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045593/state.json" "file_sha256" "2441410f40421933fd9f2904bef98dac387611ebb758a09108a0854c3954d2f0"} {"question" "q-135392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045593/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0b05af8f9449f903ce2f73e1a53348128ecd00760c96cc5043325a3b72829568"} {